<h2>The FIR and its setting</h2>
<p>The incident occurred on 8 August 2026; the FIR followed on 12 August 2026. The allegations concerned a physical altercation within a family — on the petitioner's case, a scuffle between her sisters-in-law into which the FIR drew her. The medico-legal certificate recorded the injuries as simple. Her husband, named as a co-accused, had already obtained interim anticipatory bail from the High Court a few days earlier in his own application.</p>
<p>The State opposed the petition on the footing that the FIR attributed specific acts to the petitioner. That is a common and often decisive objection — but specificity of allegation is the beginning of the inquiry, not its end.</p>

<h2>The court's checklist</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Relationship of parties</strong><p>The dispute was between known, related persons — a context where allegations can be entangled with wider family friction.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Nature of injuries</strong><p>The MLC recorded simple injuries, lowering the gravity of the alleged offences.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Delay in complaint</strong><p>Four days passed between incident and FIR — not fatal, but relevant to the urgency of custodial interrogation.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Conduct and antecedents</strong><p>No criminal record; the petitioner had joined the investigation from time to time when called.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Parity</strong><p>The co-accused husband already held interim protection in a connected application.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Investigative needs</strong><p>Nothing suggested that custodial interrogation was necessary for recoveries or confrontation.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The doctrinal frame</h2>
<p>The order grounds itself in the three pillars of anticipatory bail law — <em>Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia</em>, <em>Siddharam Satlingappa Mhetre</em> and <em>Sushila Aggarwal</em> — for the propositions that pre-arrest bail is a facet of personal liberty, that it does not blunt the investigation, and that appropriately crafted conditions can secure every legitimate investigative interest. The operative protection follows the standard Delhi form: release on bond upon arrest, movement restrictions, a live mobile number, monthly reporting to the SHO, and strict non-contact with the complainant and witnesses.</p>
<p>Equally standard — and worth remembering on both sides — is the closing caution: the observations are confined to the bail decision, express no opinion on the merits, and the State retains liberty to seek cancellation on breach.</p>

<h2>Practice notes for intra-family FIRs in Delhi</h2>
<p>First, the MLC frequently decides these applications. Simple injuries shift the case towards protection; grievous injuries or weapon use shift it away. Obtaining and reading the MLC before drafting is elementary but often skipped.</p>
<p>Second, documented cooperation is currency. Appearing before the investigating officer on notice, and keeping proof of appearance, converts an abstract undertaking into demonstrated conduct the court can rely on.</p>
<p>Third, connected applications should be coordinated. Where several family members are named, the first order — like the husband's interim protection here — sets the baseline for parity arguments in the rest. Sequencing and consistency of the defence narrative across applications matter.</p>
<p>Finally, for complainants, the decision is a reminder that anticipatory bail is neither an acquittal nor a finding that the FIR is false. The investigation continues, the chargesheet may still follow, and breach of conditions — contact, threats, non-appearance — is a concrete ground to move for cancellation.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This report is based on the order of the Delhi High Court in Preeti Singh v. State of NCT of Delhi, Bail Appln. 4096/2026, dated 29 September 2026. Anticipatory bail is now governed by Section 482 of the BNSS, which carries forward the substance of Section 438 CrPC; the settled case law under the old provision continues to apply.</p>
</div>
